#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092012 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092013 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" :question/math-academy-id "e-7186" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f07cecb-b658-5b3b-8cba-fc8183b56089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089227} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3ccb11-9bde-5497-aa8c-93ce9414643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089228} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7b5b87-0e72-5959-b794-ed035f8ec46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089229} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d6f2b1-b504-523d-9650-7659b2e6ec45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089230} {:answer/id #uuid "19979801-1f64-5840-b1ce-438e2af067dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f3ccb11-9bde-5497-aa8c-93ce9414643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089228} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc24ddc-ff1f-59d3-a31e-b340bb39ac85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089226}] :question/id #uuid "ac0c7623-4587-52d5-8de6-ad1d378c8244" :question/math-academy-id "q-12121" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2), (-1, 2), (1, 1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $R_{- π}$ by an angle of $π$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(1, 2)$\n2. $(1,-2)$\n3. $(-1,-1)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911977f9-4dd7-5952-a7d2-5e7840939b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286104} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb95726-da34-5373-afcd-2fe81b267f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286105} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4bc3d-a55e-5bbb-8cf3-be60cc08dce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286106} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7d6c0b-89d4-58f6-b838-87a105cabcb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286107} {:answer/id #uuid "1595331b-914e-55d1-ad79-8a6281f2e79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "911977f9-4dd7-5952-a7d2-5e7840939b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286104} :answer-field/id #uuid "7765a4c1-9acf-5c4a-b959-40fe56dae2b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18446b37-20cc-5e44-a427-24676c932277" :question/math-academy-id "q-12124" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,1)$\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7b110b-04b2-5dff-9776-a58956cadb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283664} {:answer/id #uuid "40dd50a4-b29a-5f50-bc4f-56a7c986dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283665} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5120bc-03c3-561a-9972-81c26fc7d824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283666} {:answer/id #uuid "ef45b474-23d4-5db1-b303-9eba3be7ca1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283667} {:answer/id #uuid "a282c8ad-012f-5852-9cf1-fb7fb0ba2903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a282c8ad-012f-5852-9cf1-fb7fb0ba2903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283668} :answer-field/id #uuid "52390b55-2534-5dcd-a08a-0142a01ed0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0af2085-c5e5-5d4f-bf4d-73318bbad63b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160371" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f73592d-1a3a-58aa-be0b-c2bf22972c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/489bc530fc35e2e740038264f2be05bffb90214249ec5602591623d457325db3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293892} {:answer/id #uuid "b5653e6b-7125-50a8-9ac0-67659ec5449a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293893} {:answer/id #uuid "65a3beed-47ed-544c-80fd-8e3487b18804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/5bd4d8cc2643767b82acc0e61d168f5b387be67a7ff96634604e0d6828cc288b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293894} {:answer/id #uuid "daa88a91-2d37-5256-bdd5-2a7499c9b507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/efb79fc1071f79597c81a0a974e7f6b21d361ac046f728625e2fe1c02bb507eb.png" :